Simu5G Framework Documentation
==============================

Building the documentation website
----------------------------------

The Simu5G documentation is built with the Sphinx tool (sphinx-doc.org), by the
doc-builder action of the omnetpp/doc-builder repository
(https://github.com/omnetpp/doc-builder/tree/v1), in the OMNeT++
doc-builder container image (ghcr.io/omnetpp/doc-builder) of the same repository. The action generates
the NED documentation with opp_docgen into neddoc/ and the C++ reference with
doxygen into api/, as reStructuredText, and then builds the site from this
directory.

To build the website locally, you need podman (or docker: ENGINE=docker), and
checkouts of omnetpp/doc-builder and INET next to the Simu5G checkout (or set
DOC_BUILDER_DIR and INET_DIR). The build runs in the common parent directory of
Simu5G and INET (ROOT), which is mounted into the container.

- Use 'make html' to build the website into _build/html, with the NED
  documentation of this working tree.
- Use 'make pdf' to also build the PDF of the User's Guide.
- Use 'make serve' to preview the built website at http://localhost:8000/
- Use 'make site' to build only the website pages, without the NED and C++
  references, in seconds instead of minutes. Their menu entries are left out,
  and :ned: names are not links. The references of an earlier 'make html' are
  removed. It needs no INET checkout.
- Use 'make live' to build only the website pages like 'make site', and serve
  them at http://localhost:8000/ (PORT=... to change the port). A page that
  you edit is rebuilt, and the browser reloads it. Ctrl-C stops it.
- Use 'make clean' to remove the generated files.

Deploying the website
---------------------

The website (https://simu5g.org) is served by GitHub Pages from the
Unipisa/Simu5G repository. It is deployed by pushing to the 'deployed-website'
branch of that repository:

$ git push <unipisa-remote> <commit>:deployed-website

The push triggers the "Build and deploy the website to GitHub Pages" workflow
(.github/workflows/sphinx-doc-build-deploy.yml). The workflow file is taken from
the pushed commit itself. Pages is only configured on the Unipisa/Simu5G
repository; pushing a 'deployed-website' branch to a fork does not deploy
anything.

The pages are built from doc/src of the pushed commit, but the NED and C++
references are generated from the sources of the latest Simu5G release, checked
out from its vX.Y.Z tag:

- on a push, it is the latest release of Unipisa/Simu5G, as reported by
  'gh release view' (drafts and pre-releases excluded), with the leading 'v'
  stripped;
- when the workflow is started manually from the Actions tab, the optional
  'doc_version' input overrides that; leave it empty for the latest release.

The INET release that the NED documentation links to is the one the
fingerprint-tests workflow of the release checks out (its 'ref: vX.Y.Z'). The
workflow replaces @VERSION@ in the NED files of the release with X.Y.Z, and
passes X.Y.Z to Sphinx as 'release' and 'version'.

So after a new release is published, merge it into 'deployed-website' and push,
or start the workflow manually.

The 'deployed-website' branch may carry commits that are not on the main
branches (e.g. site-only doc fixes). Merge the new content into it rather than
force-pushing over it, or bring those commits over first.
